Objective;

  Relay a captured NTLM authentication attempt live to a different target system,
  achieving authentication as the victim without ever cracking their password.

Why It Works:

  NTLM authentication is a challenge-response protocol.
  When a victim authenticates to an attacker-controlled resource,
  the attacker can forward that authentication handshake in real-time to a third system.
  The third system challenges the attacker, who forwards the challenge back to the victim,
  gets their response, and forwards that back — acting as a transparent man-in-the-middle.
  The result: the attacker is authenticated to the third system as the victim.

  Critical requirement: the relay destination must have SMB signing disabled or not enforced.
  SMB signing requires every message to carry a cryptographic signature derived from the session key —
  which the relay can't forge without knowing the actual secret.

Commands:
bash

 # Step 1: Disable SMB/HTTP in Responder (we want to capture, not respond)
 # Edit /etc/responder/Responder.conf: SMB = Off, HTTP = Off

 # Step 2: Start Responder for poisoning only
   sudo responder -I eth1 -wdv

 # Step 3: Start ntlmrelayx targeting the Ubuntu host (.105)
   impacket-ntlmrelayx -tf targets.txt -smb2support

  Findings:
    . jdoe's authentication successfully relayed to .105 (Ubuntu/Samba host)
    . Relay succeeded — confirmed by ntlmrelayx output showing successful authentication
    . jdoe's actual permissions on .105 determined what the relay could access (guest-level in this case)

Critical Lesson: Relay Success ≠ Privilege Granted

  Relay success means you authenticated as jdoe to the target — jdoe's real permissions on that target still apply.
  If jdoe is a guest, you're a guest. The relay gives you jdoe's identity, not admin rights.

 Detection:
   . Unusual authentication sources in Security event logs (4624/4625)
   . NTLM authentications from unexpected hosts
   . SMB signing enforcement eliminates this attack entirely
   
 Key Lessons:
   .  NetNTLMv2 can be relayed live even when it can't be cracked —
      two separate attack paths from the same captured hash
   .  SMB signing is the definitive mitigation — not optional in any serious environment
   .  Relay works best against targets without signing;
      verify signing status before selecting relay targets (netexec smb <target> --gen-relay-list)

Interview Explanation:

 "NTLM relay attacks forward a victim's live authentication handshake to a third system in real time,
  authenticating as the victim without ever knowing their password.
  I used Responder for poisoning and impacket-ntlmrelayx to relay jdoe's captured authentication to the Ubuntu host,
  achieving a successful relay. The key constraint is that the relay destination must not enforce SMB signing." 
